// A tree relation end to end (Inputs/tree.ent): a sum down the tree in a
// cascading `for` that reads its parent's sum, a component taken from the
// nearest ancestor that has it, and the order parents and children are
// visited in; through a node that changes its parent, one whose parent
// dies, and a new node in the dead one's slot.
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/tree.ent 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// Staged, fused and on all cores: the same.
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/tree.ent 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s

// The nodes are spawned leaf, mid, top, side: a pass in that order would
// give the leaf 11. The mid was connected to the side, then to the top.
// CHECK:      1: leaf 111
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: mid 110
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: lone 5
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: top 100
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: side 1100
// The leaf's parent has no Mark; the top, two up, has.
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: leaf marked 7
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: mid marked 7
// Nodes without a parent first, then the others, every parent before its
// children. The mid had a child when it got its parent, so the tree was
// built again: by the nodes' keys, each after its ancestors.
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: visit lone
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: visit top
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: visit mid
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: visit leaf
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: visit side
// The leaf is connected to the side, which is before it: it has left the
// mid, and its place is the same.
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: leaf 1101
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: mid 110
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: lone 5
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: top 100
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: side 1100
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: leaf marked 9
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: mid marked 7
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: visit lone
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: visit top
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: visit side
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: visit leaf
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: visit mid
// The side is gone: the leaf has no ancestor, so no `for` with `up`
// visits it; one that only cascades still does, where its edge puts it.
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: leaf 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: mid 110
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: lone 5
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: top 100
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: mid marked 7
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: visit lone
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: visit top
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: visit leaf
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: visit mid
// A new node takes the side's slot and not its child, and goes to the
// end. The leaf keeps the edge to the node that is gone.
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: leaf 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: mid 110
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: lone 5
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: top 100
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: fresh 2100
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: mid marked 7
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: visit leaf
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: visit lone
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: visit top
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: visit mid
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: visit fresh
// CHECK-NOT:  {{.}}

// RUN: sed 's/ tree capacity 16/ tree sorted capacity 16/' %S/Inputs/tree.ent \
// RUN:     > %t.sorted.ent
// RUN: grep -q "tree sorted" %t.sorted.ent
// RUN: %ent run %t.sorted.ent 2>/dev/null \
// RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SORTED
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %t.sorted.ent 2>/dev/null \
// RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SORTED
// The same tree with `tree sorted`: it is in two archetypes (nodes with a
// Mark and without), and each has its rows by depth: lone without a parent,
// then mid, then leaf; top, then side. Sums, marks and the order of visits
// are what they are unsorted.
// SORTED:      1: lone 5
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: mid 110
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: leaf 111
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: top 100
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: side 1100
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: mid marked 7
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: leaf marked 7
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: visit lone
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: visit top
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: visit mid
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: visit side
// SORTED-NEXT: 1: visit leaf
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: lone 5
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: mid 110
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: leaf 1101
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: top 100
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: side 1100
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: mid marked 7
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: leaf marked 9
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: visit lone
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: visit top
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: visit mid
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: visit side
// SORTED-NEXT: 2: visit leaf
// Destroying the side sorted its archetype and with it the edges: the leaf
// lost its edge at once and is a node without a parent, before the mid in
// its rows and in the visits.
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: lone 5
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: leaf 1
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: mid 110
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: top 100
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: mid marked 7
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: visit lone
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: visit leaf
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: visit top
// SORTED-NEXT: 3: visit mid
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: lone 5
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: leaf 1
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: mid 110
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: top 100
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: fresh 2100
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: mid marked 7
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: visit lone
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: visit leaf
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: visit top
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: visit mid
// SORTED-NEXT: 4: visit fresh
// SORTED-NOT:  {{.}}

// An entity that would be its own ancestor stops the program, when the
// system that connected it ends.
// RUN: %ent build %S/Inputs/tree_cycle.ent -o %t.cycle 2>/dev/null
// RUN: not --crash %t.cycle > %t.out 2>&1
// RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CYCLE --input-file=%t.out
// CYCLE:     a tree
// CYCLE-NOT: not reached
